{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "TankFuelAndVentingEquations",
    "description": "TankFuelGenerator's `AverageTankGasoline`, TVV-8's temperature/RVP adjustment, and TVV-5's two venting-equation forms. Ports docs/evap-fvv.md 2.5, 2.10 and 2.13.\n\nWHY THESE THREE ARE ONE COMPONENT. They are the three pieces of `process-evap-fvv` that the leaks and permeation slices do not already have, and two of them are joined by one number: `AverageTankGasoline.RVP` is what TVV-8 interpolates the adjustment polynomial at. Splitting them would put the producer and the only consumer that reaches MOVESOutput in different files.\n\nWHAT IS CHECKED AND WHAT IT IS CHECKED AGAINST. `averageTankGasoline` is captured EMPTY in all three evaporative snapshots -- TankFuelGenerator runs master-side and its output never reaches a SQL worker -- so there is no reference row for it, and the assertions here are against values recomputable by hand from the coefficient tables in docs/evap-fvv.md 2.5 and 2.10. What ties them to the snapshot is the fixture: `fixtures/process-evap-fvv.esm` uses this RVP and matches all 128 rows of MOVESOutput to 7.5e-06, and docs/evap-fvv.md 7.2 measures that a port which skipped this component lands 2.79 per cent high.\n\nWHY THE VENTING EQUATIONS ARE HERE AND NOT IN THE FIXTURE. docs/evap-fvv.md 0.3 measures that this fixture's `opModeFraction` for the cold-soak mode is exactly 0, so doubling TVV-5's equation leaves all 128 output cells BIT-IDENTICAL. A fixture-level check of them would pass with the equations deleted. They are therefore checked here, at probe canister loads chosen so that the blend weight and the clamp are each discriminating, and the fixture makes no claim about them at all.\n\nTHE COEFFICIENTS ARE DATA AND THE FORMS ARE TEMPLATES. `cumTVVCoeffs` holds 2,188 rows and eighteen distinct equation labels; factored, those are two forms for the venting term and one for the leak term, which live in lib/evaporative.esm as `tvv_quadratic_root`, `tvv_blend` and `tvv_leak_linear`. The eighteen coefficient rows stay data. docs/evap-fvv.md 2.10 carries the table and says where it came from, because it is the one thing the snapshot does not contain: the execution database holds the labels only, and the expressions they abbreviate live in the MOVES default database.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "moves",
      "onroad",
      "evaporative",
      "fuel",
      "tank-vapor-venting"
    ]
  },
  "models": {
    "TankFuelAndVentingEquations": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "../lib/evaporative.esm",
          "only": [
            "cubic_in",
            "knot_interpolate",
            "tank_fuel_k_gasoline",
            "tank_fuel_k_ethanol",
            "tank_fuel_gas_portion_rvp",
            "tank_fuel_reddy_rvp",
            "tank_fuel_zone_evap_temp",
            "tank_fuel_rvp_loss_ratio",
            "tvv_quadratic_root",
            "tvv_blend",
            "tvv_leak_linear",
            "tvv_cumulative_vented"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tolerance": {
        "rel": 1e-12
      },
      "variables": {
        "tfgFormulationRVP": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 8.0,
          "description": "fuelFormulation.RVP for formulation 9114, the one gasoline formulation this region supplies at a positive market share (docs/evap-fvv.md 2.5, TFG-1a). Carries RVP, in psi; declared unitless because these are empirical polynomials whose coefficients would need fractional inverse powers of it (docs/esm-conventions.md 11)."
        },
        "tfgFormulationRVPE85": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 7.7,
          "description": "fuelFormulation.RVP for formulation 27002, the E85 formulation. Carries RVP, in psi; declared unitless because these are empirical polynomials whose coefficients would need fractional inverse powers of it (docs/esm-conventions.md 11)."
        },
        "tfgEtohVolume": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 10.0,
          "description": "fuelFormulation.ETOHVolume, per cent by volume, the same for both surviving formulations. Carries per cent by volume; declared unitless because these are empirical polynomials whose coefficients would need fractional inverse powers of it (docs/esm-conventions.md 11)."
        },
        "tfgMarketShare": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 1.0,
          "description": "fuelSupply.marketShare. One formulation per fuel type at share 1, which is why this fixture cannot discriminate TFG's weighting scheme (docs/evap-fvv.md 8.1)."
        },
        "tfgYearMultiplicity": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 1.0,
          "description": "The count of Year rows sharing the fuel year whose yearID is in RunSpecYear. It multiplies every share, so it cancels out of the three ratios and does NOT cancel out of gasoholMarketShare."
        },
        "tfgZoneMinTemperature": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 59.5,
          "description": "min(zoneMonthHour.temperature) over zone 261610, month 8. Carries degrees Fahrenheit; declared unitless because these are empirical polynomials whose coefficients would need fractional inverse powers of it (docs/esm-conventions.md 11)."
        },
        "tfgZoneMaxTemperature": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 81.900001525879,
          "description": "max(zoneMonthHour.temperature) over zone 261610, month 8. The stored value is a float32 widened to double, hence the trailing digits. Carries degrees Fahrenheit; declared unitless because these are empirical polynomials whose coefficients would need fractional inverse powers of it (docs/esm-conventions.md 11)."
        },
        "tfgCommingleFactor": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 1.0,
          "description": "TFG-3a's step function at gasoholMarketShare = 1.0. The function is NOT monotonic (it peaks at 1.040 in the 0.5 band), so it is a table and is carried here as the one entry this fixture selects."
        },
